Abstract = {In times of polycrisis, the range of interconnected risks -
   environmental, societal, economical, geopolitical, technological -
   create the milieu in which we must face these challenges as nodes within
   a network. No individual one can be addressed in isolation but instead
   must be understood as relational and operating within a unified system.
   Within this context, considerations of justice are increasingly referred
   to in relation to climate change. The system-wide changes needed in
   responding to the polycrisis ensures that social inequality must be
   understood as a central tenet across any potential or aspirational
   transformative change. As such, it is important to consider what justice
   might look like with regards to how we get there, who leads the
   narrative of the future, and in what fundamental ways is it different
   from where we currently are. We aim not to separate specific justice
   narratives (e.g., climate justice, energy justice) from societal justice
   on a broader level. Instead, within this paper, the importance of
   imagination in questions of justice is outlined as it is concerned with
   responding to climate change, and the polycrisis with which it is
   entangled. Climate (in)justice acts as a useful heuristic for
   understanding normative issues that may culminate in relation to climate
   change but is not operative as a specific isolated outcome or
   orientation. Climate justice is not a possible outcome outside of wider
   societal justice. Climate change represents an unprecedented challenge
   to the human imagination in terms of the scope and scale of the impact
   on the natural world; the ability to visualise the social, political and
   economic consequences of these changes and how changing course is
   envisaged. Yet, since their foundations, questions of social justice
   have been related to processes of imagination. Within this paper, we
   explore the role of imagination in shaping attitudes and responses to
   the climate crisis, specifically examining how imaginative processes
   influence perceptions of justice and ethical considerations. The primary
   research question guiding this study is: How do imaginative processes
   contribute to shaping notions of justice in relation to the climate
   crisis?},
